v4k_8tile_10b     8    23.1         57%        53%        21%         0%         0%         0%     53%      85%

tracker = the whole tracker (base - untracked), i.e. the hard cap on any
tracker-removal design at that cell. >100% means a zero-cost tracker would
clear the 1.30x bar there.
```

## Verdict against the pre-registered rule

n = 5 complete rounds, 480 rows, **0 rows under foreign load** (9 cells were
discarded and re-run). Numbers below are stable across n=2 / n=3 / n=5.

**Answer to the question asked** — tracker ms/frame attributable to each half,
`cls_none - cls_<class>`, only where the bands are disjoint:

| cell | tile RECON (picture buffer) | FILTER CHAIN | whole tracker |
|---|---|---|---|
| 8bpc t=1 | 10.6 | **13.8** | 51.2 |
| 8bpc t=2 | 12.2 | 5.2 | 40.8 |
| 8bpc t=4 | 12.4 | null (overlap) | 24.8 |
| 8bpc t=8 | 7.3 | null (overlap) | 20.4 |
| 10bpc t=1 | 13.3 | 9.6 | 58.8 |
| 10bpc t=2 | 15.8 | 5.7 | 38.4 |
| 10bpc t=4 | 15.3 | null (overlap) | 23.3 |
| 10bpc t=8 | 12.1 | 4.8 | 19.6 |

**At t=1 the two halves are comparable** — the filter chain is actually the
LARGER of the two at 8bpc (13.8 vs 10.6). **At every t > 1 reconstruction is
2-3x the filter chain**, and the filter chain stops being separable from noise
at t=4 on both depths.

Against the rule as written:

* **A clears 1.30x at 0 of 8 cells.** Its best projection is 1.346 (8bpc t=1).
  The first clause fails outright.
* **A removes >= 25% of the excess over the bar at 7 of 8 cells** (48/44/51/33%
  at 8bpc, 24/39/58/53% at 10bpc; the miss is 10bpc t=1 at 24%), with
  `cls_recon+big` disjoint from `cls_none` at 8 of 8. The second clause passes.
* The "not worth it" clause (< 10% of the excess at every t > 1 cell) does not
  fire: A is 33-58% at every t > 1 cell.

**The number supports Design A** — but read the reason, because it is not "A is
big at t=1":

1. **A's share HOLDS across thread counts, and B's cannot.** A is 33-58% of the
   excess at every t > 1 cell. B is a t=1-only mechanism, and the bar is at
   every thread count.
2. **A is the largest single class at every cell above t=1**, by 2-3x over the
   filter chain.
3. **B has the higher ceiling at t=1 and only there.** The whole tracker is
   234% of the excess at 8bpc t=1 and 105% at 10bpc t=1, so a zero-cost tracker
   would clear the bar at both t=1 cells (and, marginally, at 8bpc t=2 and t=4:
   109% and 103%). B is a strict subset of that ceiling — it removes atomics,
   not the interval bookkeeping — and **its actual share was not measured
   here.** At 8bpc t=1 the sub-64-KiB classes B would speed up
   (recon_small ~9.3 + decode 7.2 + other 13.4 ~= 30 ms) are more than twice
   A's whole reach at that cell, so B is a good ADDITIONAL t=1 buy, not a
   substitute.
4. **Neither clears t=8 on either depth**, and neither can: a zero-cost tracker
   still leaves 1.339 (8bpc) / 1.390 (10bpc) there. Whatever closes t=8 is not
   tracker work.

Two numbers that bound A honestly:

* A's ms are a **lower bound** — the instrument branches over the tracker body
  rather than deleting it (see `exposed`, 30 ms at t=1), and at t > 1 a real
  row split would additionally delete the per-row compact COPY and its
  write-back that only exist to make per-row guards possible. That copy was
  **not measured**; do not credit it without measuring it.
* A's ms are also an **upper bound on the recon half specifically**: it assumes
  a row split reaches all of `recon+big` and all of `picwb`. `picwb` is only
  100% recon in vectors with no inter prediction and no loop restoration, which
  is what these two 4K vectors are.

## What was NOT measured

* **Design B itself.** Only its ceiling (the whole tracker at t=1). A
  `Cell`-based serial tracker's share of that 51-59 ms is unknown.
* **The per-row compact copy** that a real row split would also delete at
  t > 1. Nulling the tracker leaves the copy in place.
* **Loop restoration and inter prediction.** Both 4K gap vectors have LR off and
  are intra-only, so `mc_arm.rs` and `looprestoration*.rs` register ZERO borrows
  here. On a vector that exercises them, `picwb` becomes mixed and the filter
  class grows. This grid is structurally blind to both.
* **Any vector but 4K 8-tile**, and any thread count but 1/2/4/8.
* **x86_64.** aarch64 only.
